<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pyrgi Tablets</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 500 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrgi">Pyrgi</a> Tablets are three gold plates of about 500 BCE, two inscribed in Etruscan and one in Phoenician, found in 1964 at ancient Pyrgi, on the Tyrrhenian coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a>. They record the dedication of a temple by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thefarie-velianas">Thefarie Velianas</a>, ruler of nearby Caere, to the goddess called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Astarte</a> in the Phoenician text and Uni in the Etruscan. Though not a word-for-word translation, the bilingual text helps scholars interpret Etruscan and shows Phoenician or Punic influence in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. They are in the National Etruscan Museum, Villa Giulia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pyrgi-tablets.jpg" alt="Pyrgi Tablets" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lamine_d'oro_in_lingua_etrusca_e_fenicia_con_dedica_di_un_luogo_sacro_a_pyrgi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
